WEBVTT

1
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.362
<i>West of Arkham...</i>

2
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.530
<i>...the hills rise wild...</i>

3
00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.041
<i>...and there are valleys
with deep woods</i>

4
00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.366
<i>that no ax has ever cut.</i>

5
00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:52.601
<i>There are dark, narrow glens</i>

6
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.529
<i>where the trees
slope fantastically...</i>

7
00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.567
<i>...where thin brooklets trickle</i>

8
00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:05.001
<i>without ever having caught
the glimpse of sunlight.</i>

9
00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.682
<i>When I went into the hills
and vales</i>

10
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.888
<i>to survey
for the new reservoir,</i>

11
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:26.161
<i>they told me
the place was evil.</i>

12
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.842
<i>They told me this in Arkham,</i>

13
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.406
<i>and because
that is a very old town,</i>

14
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.329
<i>full of witch legends,</i>

15
00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.680
<i>I thought the evil
must be something</i>

16
00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.763
<i>which grandams
had whispered to children</i>

17
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.166
<i>through centuries.</i>

18
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:01.243
<i>Then I saw the dark
westward tangle of glens</i>

19
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.124
<i>and slopes for myself...</i>

20
00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:11.604
<i>...and ceased
to wonder at anything</i>

21
00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:16.202
<i>besides its own elder mystery.</i>

22
00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:56.808
Before me, Raphael.

23
00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.446
Behind me, Gabriel.

24
00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.329
To my left...

25
00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.009
...Uriel.

26
00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:14.925
And to my right, Michael.

27
00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:18.805
I call upon
the principalities of earth,

28
00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:22.890
air, fire, water, and ether.

29
00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.968
Be present with me now.

30
00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.889
Spirit of fire,

31
00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.163
continue to burn out

32
00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.328
any trace of cancer

33
00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:42.287
from the body
of Theresa Gardner, my mother.

34
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.642
Grant me your protection.

35
00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:57.128
Grant me my freedom.

36
00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:02.006
And last, not least,

37
00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:03.643
get me out of here.

38
00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:07.770
I'm sorry.
I'm sorry to intrude.

39
00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.626
- I didn't see you.
- This is private property.

40
00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:10.786
Oh, I was told
this is county land.

41
00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.530
County land ends at the river.
You're trespassing.

42
00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:17.120
Ah. They said the mayor notified
all the property owners.

43
00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:20.686
- Uh, I apologize.
- First I've heard of it.

44
00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:21.806
What now?

45
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.922
Sorry.
I'm, uh... I'm Ward Phillips.

46
00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.690
I'm doing a survey of the valley
for Hydrolit.

47
00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.203
The hydroelectric company.

48
00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:35.490
Cool story.

49
00:05:35.491 --> 00:05:39.405
Well, thanks for
ruining my ritual, Ward.

50
00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:42.923
- Ritual?
- Yes, ritual.

51
00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:46.168
You know, will, intent,
blessed be?

52
00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.805
Is it Wiccan or Alexandrian?

53
00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:52.010
Which do you think?

54
00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:54.604
Definitely Alexandrian.

55
00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.244
That's your
second mistake today.

56
00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:04.045
Stick to the east bank
and you'll find your way back.

57
00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.241
Yeah, I will.
Thanks.

58
00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.930
Hey, uh,
I didn't get your name.

59
00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.926
Lavinia Gardner.

60
00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:17.283
I live out here.
Unfortunately.

61
00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.289
- Let's go.
- Nice knowing you.

62
00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:31.889
Come on, Comet.

63
00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:21.764
Where were you?

64
00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.881
Are you trying to give
your mother a heart attack?

65
00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.026
I just thought I'd take Comet
around the trail before dinner.

66
00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:27.609
It's not a big deal.

67
00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.426
And I thought I told you
not to go without your helmet.

68
00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.106
And where are your boots?
What happened to your boots?

69
00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.486
Dad, Jesus, relax.
It's not like I'm trick riding.

70
00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:35.965
Accidents happen, Lavinny.
Someday...

71
00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.400
I'll have kids of my own,
and I'll understand.

72
00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:40.426
Exactly right. See?
Communication. Me and you, babe.

73
00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:43.170
Now get Comet back into the barn
before your mother finds out.

74
00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:44.803
Lavinia.

75
00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:49.842
Have you seen your brother?
He's been gone all afternoon.

76
00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.086
He's supposed to be studying,
as are you.

77
00:07:52.130 --> 00:07:53.803
How should I know?

78
00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:55.586
He's probably listening
to NASA recordings with Ezra.

79
00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.480
Do you mean to tell me that
your brother is smoking dope

80
00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:03.762
with that hippie reprobate,
and you didn't do anything?

81
00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.403
Lavinia?
You didn't do anything?

82
00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:06.770
- Let it go, sweetie.
- No.

83
00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:08.608
It's fine. She's fine.
Everything is fine.

84
00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:33.086
Benny, I can smell
your stoned ass from here.

85
00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:37.879
- Busted.
- You're so obvious.

86
00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:39.410
Sam!

87
00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.282
Benny's little
partner in crime.

88
00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:42.986
Hey, I found some more
of Grandpa's old stuff.

89
00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.649
- Oh, no way!
- Check this out.

90
00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:45.806
Let me see.

91
00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:47.648
Ooh! Heh.
Dibs.

92
00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.106
- No fair!
- You already got his compass.

93
00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:50.530
I need that compass.

94
00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:54.803
Oh.
Did you manage to curse us all?

95
00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:58.450
I don't do curses.

96
00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.970
They come back on you
times three.

97
00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:01.826
Now go put Comet's tack away
so we're on time for dinner,

98
00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.319
and Mom doesn't bust
any more blood vessels.

99
00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:06.566
- I'm not your barn bitch.
- Actually, you are.

100
00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:11.322
Witch. Sorceress.
Soul-sucking succubus.

101
00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.124
Hey, I met some weird guy
down by the river.

102
00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:15.809
Said he was doing a survey.

103
00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:17.648
A survey of what?

104
00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.647
The water table
or something.

105
00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:22.285
Not sure.

106
00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.844
He's kind of cute, though.

107
00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:25.119
Aw.

108
00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:40.402
What are you looking at,
Jack-Jack?

109
00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.169
Dad said
if you look long enough,

110
00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.088
you can see stars
down there.

111
00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.281
Hmm!
I wouldn't hold my breath.

112
00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.402
Dad took too much acid
back in the hippie days.

113
00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:52.522
But I do see stars!

114
00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.209
Your eyes are playing tricks.

115
00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.970
W-What are you
doing out here, anyway?

116
00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:59.400
Dad sent me for water,
but I can't untie the rope.

117
00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:00.884
Jesus.

118
00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:04.640
Why can't they just buy bottled
water like everyone else?

119
00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:10.764
Thanks for cooking again,
Dad.

120
00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.120
Aw, you know I love cooking
for you guys.

121
00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:14.524
Oh, by the smell

122
00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.926
I can tell
it's really coming a long way.

123
00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:22.002
- I miss Mom's pancakes.
- Okay.

124
00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.327
So, uh, what is for dinner?

125
00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:26.520
Cassoulet.

126
00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.766
Oh, no.
Gross.

127
00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:30.961
It's a traditional French dish.

128
00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:32.490
In other words, peasant food.

129
00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.386
Jack, can you take the
lid off the veggies, please?

130
00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.447
- Yeah.
- I think it smells great.

131
00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:37.786
That's because you're high.

132
00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.386
Shut up. We all know
you'd rather be eating

133
00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:40.466
fast food right now,
anyway.

134
00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:41.924
I love mystery meat.

135
00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.327
Well, this is not
mystery meat, dear.

136
00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.125
It's a mélange of duck,
pork, regional spices,

137
00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.162
and it just happens to be
your mother's favorite.

138
00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.202
Yeah!
I love ducks!

139
00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:55.128
Everybody loves ducks.

140
00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.685
We are down four pips,
but we didn't get oversold.

141
00:10:57.770 --> 00:11:01.081
The market came up.

142
00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.520
No, we don't want to
be selling just yet.

143
00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.402
Uh, when I took
the short position,

144
00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.919
I was assuming that we'd get
two-to-one on it.

145
00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.643
Theresa!
Dinner's ready!

146
00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.808
Look, we have
a nice ratio confluence.

147
00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.842
It's getting cold!

148
00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.246
Just take a look
at the ratio strategy I sent,

149
00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.680
and I'll call you
in an hour?

150
00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.204
Great.

151
00:11:24.331 --> 00:11:25.525
I'm coming.

152
00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:27.687
You're not gonna
have it? Really?

153
00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:30.490
Babe, can you do something
about that router?

154
00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.999
I'm gonna start
losing clients.

155
00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:36.122
It's probably the dish.
I'll take a look at it tomorrow.

156
00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:37.570
Oh, thank you.

157
00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:38.680
Hmm.

158
00:11:38.770 --> 00:11:40.887
Sam, lie down.

159
00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:42.125
Good boy.

160
00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:44.127
- What's this?
- Cassoulet.

161
00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:48.005
Although apparently our daughter
would rather have fast food.

162
00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.769
Uh, and just
what kind of meat

163
00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:51.666
do you think you're eating
at the Golden Arches?

164
00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:53.283
I know
it's mechanically retrieved,

165
00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.049
but it tastes like heaven.

166
00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.329
Then you'll know
what heaven is.

167
00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:03.410
No, I'm gonna puke.
No one eats alpacas.

168
00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:05.919
They use them
to make sweaters.

169
00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.610
I don't know why you bought them
in the first place.

170
00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.928
Because they're the animal
of the future, remember?

171
00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.087
Those Mayans knew
what they were about.

172
00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:23.386
- That's why they went extinct.
- Lavinia, stop it.

173
00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:24.560
Come on, eat up.

174
00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.363
Nathan, do you think
that you could

175
00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:30.768
go down to the basement and
choose us one of your finest?

176
00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:31.810
I know just the thing.

177
00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:42.322
Next time I'll cook.

178
00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:44.009
- It's not that bad.
- Yeah.

179
00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:10.680
There's a smell of
a conspiracy here.

180
00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:17.886
What's the matter?
Hmm?

181
00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:19.410
Nothing.
It's just...

182
00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:21.806
I just don't think
I can handle this.

183
00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:24.240
- Like, it's been...
- She's being moody.

184
00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:25.770
...a year of really...

185
00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.085
Guess what time it is?

186
00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.186
- I mean...
- No. Don't say it. Please.

187
00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:31.679
Guess what time it is.

188
00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:35.161
- Dad.
- Time for you to do the dishes!

189
00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.247
- You! You! You!
- No! Jack! Jack!

190
00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.486
- No, it's you, Jack.
- No!

191
00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:40.786
It's you, Jack.
You got to do it.

192
00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.244
Now it's Lavinia!

193
00:13:42.290 --> 00:13:44.566
No, it's not my turn.
That's not how this works.

194
00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:46.841
We have a system in this house.
It's Jack's turn.

195
00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:54.884
- Hey.
- Hi.

196
00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.525
God, you know,
I hear myself with her.

197
00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.766
I am turning into my mother.

198
00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:01.244
Oh, God.

199
00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:03.361
You couldn't be any more
different than your mother.

200
00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:04.560
Believe me.

201
00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:07.409
That's what everyone says
until they are.

202
00:14:07.450 --> 00:14:09.487
What about me?

203
00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:11.601
Living on
my father's old farm,

204
00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.484
exactly like I said
I never would.

205
00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:16.283
But it's the first thing
you've ever done

206
00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:17.684
he would have approved of.

207
00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.889
Yeah, I can still hear his
intellectually abusive voice

208
00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:24.649
in my head.

209
00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.488
"You're never gonna be
a painter, Nathan.

210
00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.806
So you can just get the fuck
out of my sight, Nathan."

211
00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:31.081
Stop it.
It'll fade.

212
00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:34.123
- Just look at this place, hmm?
- Yeah.

213
00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.129
I mean, he wouldn't even
recognize it.

214
00:14:37.170 --> 00:14:38.650
It's ours.

215
00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.925
Nathan, I...

216
00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.882
Just...

217
00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:52.082
It's been six months.
Don't you think we should try?

218
00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:53.769
I...

219
00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:55.529
I just...

220
00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:59.325
I don't know how you can
still be interested in me.

221
00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:02.962
You know I've always been
a leg man.

222
00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:09.529
So if I have my legs cut off,
would you still love me then?

223
00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:13.969
Bit kinky, but...

224
00:15:14.010 --> 00:15:16.206
Yes!

225
00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:18.526
Could tuck you into
my carry-on luggage

226
00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:20.527
and enjoy you
wherever I go.

227
00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:22.129
Ooh!
Now, that <i>is</i> sexy.

228
00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:23.570
Mmm.

229
00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:27.890
And look at this.

230
00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.525
Look at us.

231
00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:33.529
All those years in the big city,
and we finally got out.

232
00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.210
Yeah.

233
00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:37.843
We're living the dream.

234
00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:42.762
Maybe it is a dream.

235
00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:48.329
Well, a dream you dream alone
is just a dream.

236
00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:52.364
A dream you dream together
is reality.

237
00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:49.961
You are even more beautiful
to me now than ever.

238
00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.447
It's because you took
your glasses off.

239
00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:54.970
It's true.

240
00:16:58.090 --> 00:17:00.047
I feel, um...

241
00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:03.686
...tarnished.

242
00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:04.806
Honey...

243
00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:10.449
...you will always be
my golden lady.

244
00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:13.124
- Really?
- Yeah.

245
00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:14.490
I love you.

246
00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:42.447
Oh, hey, Sam.
Good boy.

247
00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.447
Good boy, Sam.

248
00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:47.210
Oh, you're not interested
in the black hole, Sam?

249
00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:48.809
Hmm?

250
00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.683
Mommy?

251
00:18:02.050 --> 00:18:03.689
Oh, baby.

252
00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:08.804
Oh, my God.
I've missed you.

253
00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:38.490
Sam?

254
00:18:51.850 --> 00:18:53.250
Mommy?

255
00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:41.443
What?!

256
00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:42.946
What the hell is that?!

257
00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:45.769
- Jack?
- Jack?

258
00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:47.403
Jack?

259
00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:50.045
Jack.
Look at Daddy.

260
00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:51.922
Jack.
Jack.

261
00:19:51.970 --> 00:19:53.610
Come here. Come here.
Come on up. Come on.

262
00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:56.770
- Look at Daddy.
- Mom?

263
00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.226
Look at Daddy.
What are you looking there?

264
00:19:58.250 --> 00:19:59.466
- Dad?
- I know.

265
00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.106
What the fuck was that?
An earthquake?

266
00:20:01.130 --> 00:20:03.106
Just watch your language.
Can you watch your language?

267
00:20:08.370 --> 00:20:09.586
God, what is that smell?

268
00:20:09.610 --> 00:20:12.762
Holy fucking shit!

269
00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.306
Benny, bring your phone.
Come on! Come on!

270
00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:15.810
Smoking like crazy.

271
00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:17.466
No, don't go near it.
Don't go near it.

272
00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:19.561
Sam, get back inside.
Get back inside.

273
00:20:19.610 --> 00:20:21.966
- I said go inside.
- What the hell is that?

274
00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:23.080
Just go inside!

275
00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:24.450
Jesus.
What a mess.

276
00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:26.083
It smells.

277
00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.928
Nate!

278
00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:31.290
There's something wrong
with Jack!

279
00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:40.681
Ohh.
It's okay, baby.

280
00:20:40.770 --> 00:20:42.045
You just had a bad dream.

281
00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:43.763
No, I think...
I think he's in shock.

282
00:20:43.810 --> 00:20:45.244
We should take him
to the hospital.

283
00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:47.646
No, it's over an hour away.
Jack, can you talk to Daddy?

284
00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:49.363
Why does it matter
how far away it is?

285
00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:51.641
- Look at him!
- What the hell happened?

286
00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:53.044
Did you guys hear it?

287
00:20:53.090 --> 00:20:54.506
It sounded like a propeller
plane having an aneurysm.

288
00:20:54.530 --> 00:20:56.999
No, it wasn't a plane.

289
00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:01.886
That smell.

290
00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.967
I don't know, like, funny.

291
00:21:04.010 --> 00:21:05.763
You can still...

292
00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:07.449
Still smell it now.

293
00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:09.049
I thought it was
a nuclear strike.

294
00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.524
The Russians and Chinese,
dude.

295
00:21:10.570 --> 00:21:12.226
- End-of-the-world shit.
- You wish.

296
00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:14.226
We'd be vaporized,
become part of the reaction,

297
00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:15.570
and convert into pure energy.

298
00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:17.886
Maybe the meteorite
sent an alien plague

299
00:21:17.930 --> 00:21:19.842
infecting the entire
fucking planet.

300
00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:21.802
Seriously apocalyptic.

301
00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:26.005
- Mommy?
- Yes, baby, I'm right here!

302
00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:28.167
- My head hurts.
- Okay, honey.

303
00:21:28.210 --> 00:21:30.600
Look, um,
let's get you upstairs.

304
00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.846
I'll put you to bed,
and you'll be okay.

305
00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:34.802
- I don't want to!
- Mm!

306
00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:36.967
No, it's okay, baby.
There's nothing to be afraid of.

307
00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:40.048
Oh, your son is sick, and this
is what you're gonna do, huh?

308
00:21:40.090 --> 00:21:43.322
Well, under the circumstances,
I don't see what else I...

309
00:21:43.370 --> 00:21:45.202
Come on.
Let's get you up.

310
00:21:45.250 --> 00:21:46.843
You can sleep with me tonight.

311
00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:50.281
In the morning
everything is gonna be okay.

312
00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:10.481
Mayor Tooma.

313
00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.840
Sheriff Pierce.
Thank you for coming.

314
00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:15.246
This better be
worth my time.

315
00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:17.566
I am so sorry
about the smell.

316
00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:19.841
Can you smell it?

317
00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:21.449
I don't smell anything.

318
00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.607
Like somebody
lit a dog on fire.

319
00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:26.688
Well, anyway,
that <i>was</i> my front yard.

320
00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:33.289
Careful.
It's still hot.

321
00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:34.605
All right.
Thanks.

322
00:22:37.611 --> 00:22:39.011
Something metallic
down here.

323
00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:40.200
What the hell happened here?

324
00:22:41.930 --> 00:22:43.762
Well, it was last night.

325
00:22:43.810 --> 00:22:45.403
I was in bed with my wife.

326
00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:49.239
It was the first time that we,
you know, since the operation,

327
00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:50.690
and then there was this boom,

328
00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:52.329
like... like a...
like a sonic boom,

329
00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:55.841
and a big flash,
like a pink light.

330
00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:58.626
Or, actually, I don't even know
what color it was.

331
00:22:58.650 --> 00:23:00.960
It wasn't like any color
I'd ever seen before,

332
00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:04.367
and then everything just blew up
or fell from the sky.

333
00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:13.843
What are you doing here?

334
00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:16.564
Think this is the result
of your little ritual yesterday?

335
00:23:16.610 --> 00:23:18.761
Ha!
If only.

336
00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:23.960
Are you looking at my legs?

337
00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:26.969
What? Oh. No. Um...
No, I was...

338
00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:29.286
Yeah, sure you weren't.
Come on.

339
00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:35.250
Who's this?

340
00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:38.044
Oh, this is that
weird guy I met by the river.

341
00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:39.490
Sorry,
what was your name again?

342
00:23:39.530 --> 00:23:40.805
Ward Phillips.

343
00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:42.409
Let me guess.
Boston.

344
00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:44.521
Providence, actually.

345
00:23:44.570 --> 00:23:46.066
He's doing a survey
of the groundwater.

346
00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:47.866
Um, isn't... isn't that what
you said yesterday?

347
00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:49.882
Yeah, yeah.
I'm a... I'm a hydrologist.

348
00:23:49.930 --> 00:23:52.286
Ward, do you have any idea
what's going on here?

349
00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:54.287
Oh, excuse me.

350
00:23:56.050 --> 00:23:57.643
- Hey, Ward.
- Hey.

351
00:23:57.690 --> 00:23:59.329
- Hey.
- Hey, I'm Benny.

352
00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:03.244
D-Don't get too close.

353
00:24:03.290 --> 00:24:06.362
Uh, looks like a meteorite.

354
00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:08.686
Usually they disintegrate
before they hit the ground,

355
00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:10.124
but not always.

356
00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:12.685
A meteorite?
Whoa.

357
00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:16.081
Sheriff, we need to get
some photographs.

358
00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:18.520
Nathan, would you mind
if I bring some press next time?

359
00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:19.799
This can be great for tourism.

360
00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:21.330
I'd just as soon
not publicize it.

361
00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:23.043
Mayor, wait.

362
00:24:23.090 --> 00:24:25.146
Is there a doctor around here
who's open on weekends?

363
00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:26.524
My son isn't feeling well...

364
00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:28.527
You can try the hospital
in Arkham.

365
00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:30.687
They usually have somebody
on the emergency desk.

366
00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:33.604
- Isn't there anywhere closer?
- Welcome to life in the sticks.

367
00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:35.490
You know, you should have
sold this place to me

368
00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:38.045
Bye-bye.

369
00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:42.290
Isn't it insane?

370
00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:45.283
Check it out.

371
00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:46.810
Hurry!

372
00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:52.803
Lavinia, can you go back inside
and check on your mother?

373
00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:53.810
Dad!

374
00:24:53.811 --> 00:24:55.881
It's okay.
We got this covered.

375
00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:56.930
Off you go.

376
00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:03.806
- See you later.
- Yeah.

377
00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:06.763
Why don't you
go check on her?

378
00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:07.810
- Be careful.
- Okay.

379
00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:11.443
It's cooling off
really fast now.

380
00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:12.810
Is that normal?

381
00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.481
Smoke coming out of it,
crazy color.

382
00:25:17.530 --> 00:25:19.442
- It was so cool.
- Okay.

383
00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:20.786
You ever seen anything
like this before?

384
00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:21.810
Um...

385
00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:27.681
Hey.
Who's that guy?

386
00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:29.409
Who?

387
00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.681
Um, Ward something.

388
00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:33.887
- He's a hydrologist.
- Oh.

389
00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:36.968
- You like him, huh?
- What? No.

390
00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:38.239
Why would you say that?

391
00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:40.247
Oh, well, come on.
It was obvious.

392
00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:43.010
You were practically
throwing yourself at him.

393
00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:44.643
- I mean, honey...
- Mom.

394
00:25:44.690 --> 00:25:46.066
Think... Look at the way
you're dressed.

395
00:25:46.090 --> 00:25:48.400
Think of the signals
you're giving out.

396
00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:49.600
Oh, God.

397
00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:52.449
Do you think it's radioactive
like kryptonite?

398
00:25:52.490 --> 00:25:54.641
Christ, I hope not.

399
00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:56.363
Well, what about, like,
an alien virus?

400
00:25:56.410 --> 00:25:57.810
I mean, it's from space,
right?

401
00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:02.126
than ordinary rocks.

402
00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:04.526
Well, do you think
it could be valuable?

403
00:26:04.570 --> 00:26:06.880
Now, that's something
I hadn't considered.

404
00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:11.162
Well, if you don't mind,
it's time we milked the alpacas.

405
00:26:16.010 --> 00:26:17.410
Milk the what?

406
00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.644
It's not like
milking a goat.

407
00:26:22.690 --> 00:26:25.000
You don't get a lot of milk
from an alpaca.

408
00:26:25.050 --> 00:26:29.124
It takes great patience
and technique.

409
00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:33.881
And, of course, you have to be
very gentle with the, uh, boobs.

410
00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:37.808
But once you get them
warmed up...

411
00:26:37.850 --> 00:26:40.445
Oh.
Nice one, dad.

412
00:26:40.490 --> 00:26:45.042
Yeah, I find mixing
a little fennel into their feed

413
00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:49.642
increases the yield
and improves the flavor.

414
00:26:50.970 --> 00:26:52.266
- Would you like a taste?
- Oh, no.

415
00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:54.646
Um, lactose intolerant.

416
00:26:57.290 --> 00:26:58.565
Your loss.

417
00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:04.326
Sweetie?

418
00:27:10.250 --> 00:27:12.003
Can you open the door, please?

419
00:27:12.050 --> 00:27:14.610
I'm so sorry.

420
00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:18.610
I-I don't... I don't know
what came over me.

421
00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:25.649
Lavinia, come on.
Please open the door.

422
00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:29.479
Uh, is there anyone else
living on the property,

423
00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:31.249
aside from your family?

424
00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.803
There's Ezra, our squatter,
but he keeps to himself.

425
00:27:35.850 --> 00:27:37.330
I haven't seen him in months.

426
00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:38.770
Would it be okay
if I talk with him?

427
00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:41.041
You can try. I mean,
Benny can show you the way.

428
00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:42.319
Yeah.
Yeah, for sure.

429
00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:44.050
But I can't promise
he's gonna talk to you.

430
00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:50.563
He's, um, special.

431
00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.681
Looks like
there's a storm coming in.

432
00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:02.965
Yeah.

433
00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:06.689
Are those cameras?

434
00:28:06.770 --> 00:28:09.444
Yeah. Ezra used to be
an electrician back in the day.

435
00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:10.666
He's got this whole place
wired up.

436
00:28:10.690 --> 00:28:12.568
Oh.

437
00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.442
Where's his juice come from?

438
00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:17.210
Solar.
Totally off the grid.

439
00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.207
Hey!
Ezra!

440
00:28:19.250 --> 00:28:20.889
You got a visitor!

441
00:28:24.370 --> 00:28:26.601
Oh!
It's the surveyor man!

442
00:28:28.050 --> 00:28:30.042
I'm sorry.
I don't think we've met yet.

443
00:28:30.090 --> 00:28:31.683
- I'm, uh...
- The hydrologist.

444
00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:34.126
Yeah.

445
00:28:34.170 --> 00:28:35.809
Pretty well informed.

446
00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:39.764
Lot of little birdies flitting
around here in this ecosphere.

447
00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:41.927
You just got to know
how to decode them.

448
00:28:41.970 --> 00:28:43.404
Come on in.

449
00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:45.328
We'll talk about
the space metal.

450
00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:50.922
- Want a hit?
- Oh, no, thank you.

451
00:28:50.970 --> 00:28:53.041
I didn't think so.

452
00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:54.604
Hey, some java?

453
00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:55.766
- Yeah.
- All right.

454
00:28:56.571 --> 00:28:59.689
Aw.
What's her name?

455
00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:02.080
G-spot.
- What?

456
00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.850
What? Come on. That's
like the coolest name ever.

457
00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:08.247
A pussy named G-spot?
It's totally hilarious.

458
00:29:08.290 --> 00:29:11.442
Hey.
Come here, lady.

459
00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:12.490
Hi.

460
00:29:12.491 --> 00:29:13.891
All right.

461
00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:16.968
Oh, yeah, you like this?
You like that?

462
00:29:17.010 --> 00:29:19.366
Hey, my apologies
for the Adam's ale.

463
00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:22.528
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

464
00:29:22.570 --> 00:29:25.529
Yeah, the H2O has gone
a little brackish.

465
00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:32.248
Yeah, I can't...
I can't smell anything.

466
00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:34.168
It's probably just rust
from the taps.

467
00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:36.805
Have you tried running the
faucet, see if it runs clear?

468
00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:40.127
Hey, that, my friend, is
straight from the mother's tit.

469
00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:43.129
Yeah, we've had some
problems with the well before

470
00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:44.365
but only in the summer.

471
00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:45.685
Something about the aquifer.

472
00:29:45.770 --> 00:29:47.921
Right.
Yeah.

473
00:29:47.970 --> 00:29:50.485
That's to do with the level
of the water table, but...

474
00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:52.690
I don't know. Shouldn't be
happening this time of year.

475
00:29:54.530 --> 00:29:56.044
Freshest water
in the department.

476
00:29:56.090 --> 00:29:59.049
That's what Dad always says.

477
00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:00.365
I'm sure it's fine,

478
00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.481
but if you want
I could run a test on a sample.

479
00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:04.362
I have all my equipment
back up in the tent.

480
00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:06.970
So, you know,
better safe than sorry.

481
00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:09.081
Hey, knock yourself out.

482
00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.482
All right!

483
00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.169
Damn, that was close.

484
00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:20.679
Tell it, baby.
Tell it, tell it!

485
00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:32.002
It's so beautiful.

486
00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.047
Lavinny, what are you doing?

487
00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:43.447
Come on.
Let's go back inside.

488
00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:53.804
It's drawing the lightning.

489
00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:27.245
<i>In my next term
as your mayor,</i>

490
00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:30.886
<i>we will be undertaking the
largest infrastructure project</i>

491
00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:33.399
<i>in our city's history
with the construction</i>

492
00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:35.919
<i>of the freshwater reservoir
on the Miskatonic...</i>

493
00:31:35.970 --> 00:31:38.883
<i>The economic impact alone...</i>

494
00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:57.561
Hello?

495
00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:02.084
Hello?

496
00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:06.050
Who is that?

497
00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:06.124
What the hell?

498
00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:32.250
How can something that big
just disappear?

499
00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:34.964
I don't know, but I'm glad.

500
00:34:39.370 --> 00:34:41.327
Did you plant those?

501
00:34:42.650 --> 00:34:46.803
No.
Maybe they're perennials.

502
00:34:51.970 --> 00:34:54.280
Great.
Here comes the circus.

503
00:35:02.570 --> 00:35:03.686
Baby.

504
00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.186
<i>Could you please tell us
exactly what happened?</i>

505
00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:13.386
<i>Well, there was a boom.</i>

506
00:35:13.410 --> 00:35:14.924
<i>Uh, it was a couple nights ago.</i>

507
00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.927
<i>A boom and a flash of light
and a vibration,</i>

508
00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:21.010
<i>and we came out,
and we saw this large rock.</i>

509
00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:23.929
Horrible. Hideous.
Oh, my God.

510
00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.121
Couldn't somebody have given me
a comb? Jesus!

511
00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:27.906
<i>I'm here with Arkham resident
Nathan Gardner,</i>

512
00:35:27.930 --> 00:35:29.762
<i>who claims that
an unidentified flying object</i>

513
00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:31.066
<i>landed in his front yard
last night.</i>

514
00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:32.410
"UFO witness"?

515
00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:34.646
You said UFO!
I didn't say UFO!

516
00:35:34.690 --> 00:35:35.760
Oh, for fuck's sake.

517
00:35:35.810 --> 00:35:38.370
Theresa, can you
get out here, please?

518
00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:43.088
Did you see a UFO?
I didn't see any UFO.

519
00:35:50.250 --> 00:35:51.445
<i>Right, and, uh...</i>

520
00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:53.004
<i>We had
a top hydrologist here...</i>

521
00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:54.484
<i>not a geologist...</i>

522
00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:56.328
Yeah, I think
the freaked-out-abductee look

523
00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:57.170
suits you pretty well.

524
00:35:57.171 --> 00:35:58.171
<i>Very good.</i>

525
00:35:58.210 --> 00:35:59.485
<i>So the meteorite,</i>

526
00:35:59.530 --> 00:36:01.249
<i>which happened to
mysteriously disappear</i>

527
00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:02.929
<i>before the time
my team and I got here.</i>

528
00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:05.166
<i>How does an object
of that size...</i>

529
00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:07.247
Theresa, can you please
get out here?

530
00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:08.770
<i>Mr. Gardner, were you sober</i>

531
00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:12.008
<i>at the time
that this event happened?</i>

532
00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.201
<i>Uh, well, I mean,
I like a bit of bourbon.</i>

533
00:36:14.250 --> 00:36:16.321
<i>Um, uh,
it comes out of Texas.</i>

534
00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:18.566
Oh, for fuck's sake.

535
00:36:18.610 --> 00:36:20.488
<i>I was... I was not...
The night before,</i>

536
00:36:20.530 --> 00:36:22.283
<i>I had a few drinks,
but I wasn't...</i>

537
00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:23.650
<i>I was sober when I came out.</i>

538
00:36:25.450 --> 00:36:26.906
- Oh, man.
- Jack, go get your mother.

539
00:36:26.930 --> 00:36:29.161
Okay.

540
00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:33.204
What part of "meteorite"
do you not understand?

541
00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:46.769
Mom.

542
00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:48.466
<i>I wouldn't classify that
as mental illness.</i>

543
00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:50.163
<i>WARK News.</i>

544
00:36:50.210 --> 00:36:52.679
Daddy!
Mommy's hurt!

545
00:36:52.770 --> 00:36:53.806
Baby?

546
00:36:55.130 --> 00:36:56.610
